{"cuisine/american-jewish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/american-jewish-cuisine","n":"American Jewish cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["American Jewish cuisine comprises the food, cooking, and dining customs associated with American Jews. It was heavily influenced by the cuisine of Jewish immigrants who came to the United States from Eastern Europe around the turn of the 20th century. It was further developed in unique ways by the immigrants and their descendants, especially in New York City and other large metropolitan areas of the northeastern U.S."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"american-jewish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Jewish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Jewish Cooking in America","ref":"cookbook/jewish-cooking-in-america","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Joan Nathan, 1994","note":"History and recipes of Jewish home cooking across the United States.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"The Gefilte Manifesto: New Recipes for Old World Jewish Foods","ref":"cookbook/the-gefilte-manifesto-new-recipes-for-old-world-jewish-foods","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Jeffrey Yoskowitz and Liz Alpern, 2016","note":"Ashkenazi deli and home staples updated by the founders of The Gefilteria.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Lox","dish/lox"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cured salmon","ingredient/cured-salmon"],["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/0a/Lox-and-bagel-02.jpg/500px-Lox-and-bagel-02.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Kristina D.C. Hoeppner","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lox-and-bagel-02.jpg"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-commander-islands":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-commander-islands","n":"Cuisine of Commander Islands","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Russia","intro":[["Cuisine of Commander Islands is a mixture of traditional Russian cuisine and cuisine of Aleut people, who inhabit the Commander Islands. Commander cuisine is largely based on fish meat, seafood, mushrooms, meat of sea mammals (seals, whales), game meat and vegetable (potato, onion, garlic, carrot) which was brought to the Commander island by Russians."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-commander-islands"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Commander_Islands"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/6d/Fish_pie.JPG/500px-Fish_pie.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Shuvaev","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fish_pie.JPG"}},"cuisine/christian-dietary-laws":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/christian-dietary-laws","n":"Christian diet","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Religious cuisine or diet","intro":[["Christian dietary laws vary between denominations. The general dietary restrictions specified for Christians in the New Testament are to \"abstain from food sacrificed to idols, from blood, from meat of strangled animals\". The early Christian Didache specifies that Christians should \"not eat meat sacrificed to idols.\" Some Christian denominations forbid certain foods during periods of fasting, which in some cases may cover half the year and may exclude meat, fish, dairy products, and olive oil."],["Christians in the Catholic, Lutheran, Anglican, and Orthodox denominations, among others, traditionally observe Friday as a meat-free day (in mourning of the crucifixion of Jesus); many also fast and abstain from consuming meat on Wednesday (in memory of the betrayal of Jesus). There are various fasting periods, most notably the liturgical season of Lent. A number of Christian denominations forbid alcohol consumption, and all Christian churches condemn drunkenness as being a sin."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_dietary_laws"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"When You Fast: Recipes for Lenten Seasons","ref":"cookbook/when-you-fast-recipes-for-lenten-seasons","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Catherine Mandell, 2023","note":"About 200 Eastern Orthodox fasting recipes without meat, and options without dairy or oil, from St Vladimir's Seminary Press.","url":"https://svspress.com/when-you-fast-recipes-for-lenten-seasons","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780881412628"}]},{"h":"Within Christian diet cooking","count":6,"links":[["Catholic diet","cuisine/fasting-and-abstinence-in-the-catholic-church"],["Coptic Orthodox diet","cuisine/fasting-and-abstinence-of-the-coptic-orthodox-church"],["Ethiopian Orthodox diet","cuisine/fasting-and-abstinence-in-the-ethiopian-orthodox-tewahedo-church"],["Goan Catholic cuisine","cuisine/goan-catholic-cuisine"],["Mangalorean Catholic cuisine","cuisine/mangalorean-catholic-cuisine"],["Mennonite cuisine","cuisine/mennonite-cuisine"]]}],"cont":null,"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2c/Peter%27s_vision_of_the_sheet_with_animals.jpg/500px-Peter%27s_vision_of_the_sheet_with_animals.jpg","w":500,"h":393,"by":"Illustrator of Henry Davenport Northrop's 'Treasures of the Bible', 1894","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter's_vision_of_the_sheet_with_animals.jpg"}},"dish/abbacchio":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/abbacchio","n":"Abbacchio","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Lazian or Roman","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Abbacchio is an Italian preparation of lamb typical of the Roman cuisine. It is consumed throughout central Italy as an Easter and Christmas dish. Abbacchio is a product protected by the European Union with the PGI mark."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-abbacchio"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbacchio"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Lazian or Roman","cuisine/roman-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"]]},{"h":"More from Lazian or Roman","links":[["Cacio e pepe","dish/cacio-e-pepe"],["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"],["Pinsa (bread)","dish/pinsa-bread"],["Roman pizza","dish/roman-pizza"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/33/Abbacchio_Pasquale.jpg/500px-Abbacchio_Pasquale.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"masolino / Tommaso Passi","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbacchio_Pasquale.jpg"}},"ingredient/puff-pastry":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/puff-pastry","n":"Puff pastry","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 15 dishes across 13 cuisines","intro":[["Puff pastry, also known as pâte feuilletée, is a light, flaky pastry, its base dough (détrempe) composed of wheat flour and water. Butter or other solid fat (beurrage) is then layered into the dough. The dough is repeatedly rolled and folded, rested, re-rolled and folded, encasing solid butter between each resulting layer."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-puff-pastry"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puff_pastry"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":13,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Canapé","dish/canape"],["Mille-feuille","dish/mille-feuille"],["Pithivier","dish/pithivier"],["Tarte Tatin","dish/tarte-tatin"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Canapé","dish/canape"],["Mille-feuille","dish/mille-feuille"],["Pithivier","dish/pithivier"],["Tarte Tatin","dish/tarte-tatin"]]],["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",[["Jésuite","dish/jesuite"],["Rogel (cake)","dish/rogel-cake"]]],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Banbury cake","dish/banbury-cake"],["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"]]],["Algerian cuisine","cuisine/algerian-cuisine",[["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"]]],["Basque cuisine","cuisine/basque-cuisine",[["Pantxineta","dish/pantxineta"]]],["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine",[["Maids of honour tart","dish/maids-of-honour-tart"]]],["Haitian cuisine","cuisine/haitian-cuisine",[["Haitian patty","dish/haitian-patty"]]],["Irish cuisine","cuisine/irish-cuisine",[["Sausage roll","dish/sausage-roll"]]],["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine",[["Ziva (dish)","dish/ziva-dish"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Sfogliatella","dish/sfogliatella"]]],["Maltese cuisine","cuisine/maltese-cuisine",[["Pastizz","dish/pastizz"]]],["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",[["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Frangipane","ingredient/frangipane"],["Powdered sugar","ingredient/powdered-sugar"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Custard","ingredient/custard"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Nutmeg","ingredient/nutmeg"],["Zante currant","ingredient/zante-currant"],["Zest","ingredient/zest"],["Cracker","ingredient/cracker"],["Paste","ingredient/paste"],["Spread","ingredient/spread"],["Toast","ingredient/toast"],["Cheese curds","ingredient/cheese-curds"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/90/Pate_feuilletee_2.jpg/500px-Pate_feuilletee_2.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pate_feuilletee_2.jpg"}},"ingredient/honey":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/honey","n":"Honey","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 18 dishes across 16 cuisines","intro":[["Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants (primarily floral nectar) or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-honey"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":16,"groups":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Lekach","dish/lekach"],["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"],["Teiglach","dish/teiglach"]]],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Candy","dish/candy"],["Medovukha","dish/medovukha"],["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"]]],["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",[["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"],["Zaazaa","dish/zaazaa"]]],["Algerian cuisine","cuisine/algerian-cuisine",[["Mhancha","dish/mhancha"]]],["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Medovukha","dish/medovukha"]]],["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Char siu","dish/char-siu"]]],["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Pepernoot","dish/pepernoot"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Gozinaki","dish/gozinaki"]]],["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine",[["Kalitsounia","dish/kalitsounia"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Tandoori chicken","dish/tandoori-chicken"]]],["Korean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine",[["Hotteok","dish/hotteok"]]],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Medovukha","dish/medovukha"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Turrón","dish/turron"]]],["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Basler Läckerli","dish/basler-lackerli"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Natural honey","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":1918134,"top":[["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",460200],["Turkey","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",105819],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",96888],["Ethiopia","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine",85412],["Iran","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",76620],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",71304],["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",65576],["Ukraine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",62215],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",61102],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",60429],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",59206],["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",38059],["Tanzania","cuisine/tanzanian-cuisine",31799],["Romania","cuisine/romani-cuisine",30712],["Spain","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",30516]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Candied fruit","ingredient/candied-fruit"],["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Hazelnut","ingredient/hazelnut"],["Kirsch","ingredient/kirsch"],["Sweetener","ingredient/sweetener"],["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fermented bean curd","ingredient/fermented-bean-curd"],["Five-spice powder","ingredient/five-spice-powder"],["Fortified wine","ingredient/fortified-wine"],["Hoisin sauce","ingredient/hoisin-sauce"],["Maltose","ingredient/maltose"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/500px-Runny_hunny.jpg","w":500,"h":758,"by":"Scott Bauer, USDA ARS","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Runny_hunny.jpg"}},"ingredient/kalpasi":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/kalpasi","n":"Kalpasi","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Parmotrema perlatum, commonly known as the powdered ruffle lichen, is a common species of foliose lichen in the family Parmeliaceae. The species has a cosmopolitan distribution and occurs throughout the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. Parmotrema perlatum is a prominent and widely recognised species within its genus across primarily temperate zones, preferring humid, oceanic-suboceanic habitats. It is found in diverse geographic areas including Africa, North and South America, Asia, Australasia, Europe, and islands in the Atlantic and Pacific oceans. It usually grows on bark, but occasionally occurs on siliceous rocks, often among mosses."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-kalpasi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalpasi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Chicken Chettinad","dish/chicken-chettinad"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5d/Parmotrema_perlatum_163808872.jpg/500px-Parmotrema_perlatum_163808872.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Michel Langeveld","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parmotrema_perlatum_163808872.jpg"}},"dish/doubanjiang":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/doubanjiang","n":"Doubanjiang","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sichuan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Doubanjiang, also known as douban, toban-djan, broad bean chili sauce, or fermented chili bean paste, is a hot and savory Chinese bean paste made from fermented broad beans (fava beans), chili peppers, soybeans, salt, and flour. Characteristically used in Sichuan cuisine, it has been called \"the soul of Sichuan cuisine.\" Sichuan dishes such as mapo tofu, huoguo (Sichuan hotpot), yuxiang flavoring, and shuizhu all use doubanjiang as a key ingredient. Other regions have their own versions: in Guangdong and Taiwan, for instance, Sichuan doubanjiang is called la-doubanjiang (Chinese: 辣豆瓣醬, \"la\" (辣) meaning \"hot\" or \"spicy\") to distinguish it from plainer  and milder versions."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-doubanjiang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doubanjiang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sichuan cuisine","cuisine/sichuan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"China Sichuan Food","ref":"site/chinasichuanfood-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chinasichuanfood.com","note":"Elaine's blog of Chinese home recipes with a strong Sichuan focus.","url":"https://www.chinasichuanfood.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"The Mala Market Blog","ref":"site/blog-themalamarket-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"blog.themalamarket.com","note":"Recipes and ingredient guides from a US importer of Sichuan pantry staples.","url":"https://blog.themalamarket.com/mala-market-blog-about-page/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Sichuan cuisine","links":[["Fish with pickled mustard greens","dish/fish-with-pickled-mustard-greens"],["Kuanfen noodles","dish/kuanfen-noodles"],["Laziji","dish/laziji"],["Suan la fen","dish/suan-la-fen"],["Yuxiang shredded pork","dish/yuxiang-shredded-pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e9/Doubanjiang.jpg/500px-Doubanjiang.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Badagnani","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doubanjiang.jpg"}},"dish/food-for-the-gods":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/food-for-the-gods","n":"Food for the gods","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Food for the gods, sometimes known as a date bar or date and walnut bar, is a Filipino pastry dessert similar to the American dessert bar. Dates and walnuts are some of the main ingredients. The food is popular during the Christmas season, when they are wrapped in colored cellophane and sometimes given as gifts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-food-for-the-gods"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Food_for_the_gods"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao"],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Panlasang Pinoy","ref":"site/panlasangpinoy-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"panlasangpinoy.com","note":"Vanjo Merano's large library of Filipino recipes and cooking videos.","url":"https://panlasangpinoy.com/filipino-food/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Kawaling Pinoy","ref":"site/kawalingpinoy-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kawalingpinoy.com","note":"Lalaine Manalo's blog of Filipino home cooking.","url":"https://www.kawalingpinoy.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Flavours of Iloilo","ref":"site/flavoursofiloilo-blogspot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"flavoursofiloilo.blogspot.com","note":"Blog of Ilonggo home dishes from Iloilo in the Western Visayas.","url":"https://flavoursofiloilo.blogspot.com/2013/05/how-about-some-chicken-binakol.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Filipino cuisine","links":[["Adobo (Filipino dish)","dish/adobo-filipino-dish"],["Aparon","dish/aparon"],["Banana ketchup","dish/banana-ketchup"],["Champorado","dish/champorado"],["Filipino barbecue","dish/filipino-barbecue"],["Isaw","dish/isaw"],["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"],["Kadyos, manok, kag ubad","dish/kadyos-manok-kag-ubad"],["Kumukunsi","dish/kumukunsi"],["Mango float","dish/mango-float"],["Palapa (condiment)","dish/palapa-condiment"],["Pastil","dish/pastil"],["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"],["Pilipit","dish/pilipit"],["Piyanggang manok","dish/piyanggang-manok"],["Sushi bake","dish/sushi-bake"],["Tortang talong","dish/tortang-talong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d2/Food_for_the_Gods_1.JPG/500px-Food_for_the_Gods_1.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"BrokenSphere","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Food_for_the_Gods_1.JPG"}},"dish/goxua":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/goxua","n":"Goxua","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Basque cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Goxua (which means \"sweet\" in Basque) is a typical dessert from the Basque Country, especially from Vitoria-Gasteiz. Jose Murguia from La Peña Dulce invented the original recipe of goxua “1”; the pastry chef from Mirandese Alberto Bornachea affirms that goxua was invented by his father trying to copy crema catalana, baptizing the resulting dessert as cazuelita."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-goxua"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goxua"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Basque cuisine","cuisine/basque-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"]]},{"h":"More from Basque cuisine","links":[["Basque cheesecake","dish/basque-cheesecake"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Gilda (snack)","dish/gilda-snack"],["Intxaursaltsa","dish/intxaursaltsa"],["Pantxineta","dish/pantxineta"],["Pincho","dish/pincho"],["Piperade","dish/piperade"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Sukalki","dish/sukalki"],["Tuna pot","dish/tuna-pot"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8e/Goxua.jpg/500px-Goxua.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Boca Dorada","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goxua.jpg"}},"dish/leberkase":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/leberkase","n":"Leberkäse","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 5 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["in Austria and the Swabian, Bavarian and Franconian parts of Germany, 'leverkaas' in the Netherlands and Fleischkäse (\"meat-cheese\"; rarely also Fleischkäs) in Saarland, Baden, Switzerland and Tyrol) is a speciality food found in the south of Germany, in Austria and parts of Switzerland. It consists of beef, pork and bacon and is made by grinding the ingredients very finely and then baking it as a loaf in a bread pan until it has a crunchy brown crust. Variations may be made using other kinds of meat such as horse meat or turkey, or may contain additional ingredients such as cheese or minced chili peppers. Liver is not traditionally considered an essential ingredient."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-leberkase"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leberk%C3%A4se"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Austrian cuisine","cuisine/austrian-cuisine"],["Bavarian cuisine","cuisine/bavari